#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.10"
# dependencies = []
# ///
"""Unit tests for the browser-free half of the demo-video recorder.

    tests/unit                  # run the suite
    tests/unit --fault-inject   # prove the suite can fail

**`dependencies = []` is the point of this file, not an accident.** Everything
tested here — the coverage report, the timeline's two renderings, the content
and opening measurements, the merge a stitch performs, the two secret types —
is a function of documents and values. None of it drives a browser. Until
issue #139 it was nonetheless unreachable without one, because `core` imported
Playwright at module scope and `__init__` imported both recorders eagerly, so
the recorder's only check was `tests/smoke`: 12,369 lines, Chromium and ffmpeg
required, ten minutes, one suite at a time behind an exclusive `flock`.

That cost is what issue #136 measures, and it is why the fault-injection rule
is *executed* for `tests/ci-unit` and *prose* for the recorder. At 12-620 s per
injection under a lock nobody maintains a manifest. At 0.2 s they do.

So this suite exists to make the discipline affordable where it was not, and
it carries its own `INJECTIONS` table with the same contract `tests/ci-unit`
uses: each entry breaks one named thing, the suite must go red, and the
**named** tests must be the ones that fired. An injection whose search string
does not match exactly once aborts the run rather than reporting a pass.

What this suite does NOT cover is stated at the bottom of this file. Read it
before treating a green run as coverage of the recorder.
"""

